text:
Loading the STECF economic table
The economic file reports one row per fleet segment, year and economic variable. Inactive fleets are removed first because they land nothing and would otherwise distort the fleet averages. The year filter keeps the panel through reference_year and drops 2024, which cannot be valued.

stecf_economic &lt;- readRDS(stecf_economic_file) |&gt;
  filter(fishing_tech != "INACTIVE", year &lt;= reference_year) |&gt;
  drop_stecf_admin_columns()

Net economic result by fleet
The resource rent approach values the service as the economic surplus of the fleet, that is total income less total costs (Eq. 3 of the methods report). STECF splits its economic variables into groups; we keep only Expenditure and Income, sign the expenditure negatively so that both can be summed in a single column, and aggregate to one net result per fleet segment and year.
Missing values become zero and zero rows are then dropped. That looks circular, and it is deliberate: STECF uses a blank to mean “not reported” and the assessment treats a non-reported item as absent rather than as a true zero, so both end up excluded from the sum.

net_economic_by_fleet &lt;- function(data) {
  data |&gt;
    filter(variable_group %in% c("Expenditure", "Income")) |&gt;
    mutate(value = replace_na(value, 0)) |&gt;
    filter(value != 0) |&gt;
    mutate(money = if_else(variable_group == "Expenditure", -value, value)) |&gt;
    summarise(
      total_expenditure = sum(money[variable_group == "Expenditure"]),
      total_income = sum(money[variable_group == "Income"]),
      total_net = total_income + total_expenditure,
      .by = c(year, fs_name)
    )
}

fleet_net &lt;- net_economic_by_fleet(stecf_economic)

An ecosystem service cannot have a negative flow, because that would describe a disservice. Box 2 of the methods report therefore splits the fleets in two: those with a positive net result carry the valuation, and those with a negative or zero result are treated as missing and recovered later from average prices.

fleet_net_positive &lt;- fleet_net |&gt;
  filter(total_net &gt; 0)

Loading and cleaning landings
The landings file reports quantities in several units. Only kilograms are relevant here, and rows with a zero quantity carry no information. We rename value to landed_kg immediately, because three different quantities are called value at various points of the original script and the name stops carrying meaning. The same year &lt;= reference_year bound used on the economic file keeps the two sides of the join on the same years.

landings_raw &lt;- readRDS(stecf_landings_file) |&gt;
  filter(year &lt;= reference_year) |&gt;
  drop_stecf_admin_columns() |&gt;
  filter(unit == "kg", value != 0) |&gt;
  rename(landed_kg = value)

Matching landings to fleet economics
Landings and fleet economics meet on the fleet segment name and the year. The join is many-to-one because a fleet has one net result but many landing records. Records that find no positive net result are the gap set of Box 2; they keep their quantities and are valued later from average prices.

landings_matched &lt;- landings |&gt;
  left_join(
    fleet_net_positive,
    by = join_by(fs_name, year),
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  )

landings_valued_input &lt;- landings_matched |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(total_net))

landings_without_rent &lt;- landings_matched |&gt;
  filter(is.na(total_net))

Joining results to the marine regions
Geometry lives in data/gisdata/marine_regions.gpkg and stays static. The published GeoPackage holds one copy of each polygon, with one column per year for landings, resource rent and price. Species, country and fleet belong in the RDS and Excel files, not in the geometry. Duplicating the polygons once per year was what made the first export large; three separate files, one per metric, would triplicate the geometry for no gain. QGIS users style tonnes_2023, rent08_k_eur_2018 or price08_eur_kg_2023 on the same layer. Nominal rent and price columns are kept beside the 2008-euro ones.
Two layers are published. The macro layer is the four pan-European sea regions; it joins on Sea_name after a label recode. The division layer is the 58 FAO divisions and subdivisions; it joins on F_CODE, or on F_DIVISION when that code is unique to one feature, which is how the collapsed NEAO recodes find the dissolved polygons. Price is the quantity-weighted average, revenue over live weight, not a sum of prices.
Mediterranean accounts use GFCM sub-areas (sa1 to sa27) while the geometry is FAO 37.x.x, so the GSA lookup is applied before the join. Black Sea sub-areas sa28 to sa30 are not in that workbook; they are mapped to Marmara, the Black Sea and the Azov Sea following the GFCM numbering. ROW cannot appear on a European map. In 2023 it was 21 percent of tonnage, so any map needs that reconciliation in mind. Legacy result columns on the source layers (Tonnes_23, Money_23 and the truncated overuse names) are not copied.

black_sea_to_fao &lt;- tribble(
  ~sub_reg_clean, ~fao_division,
  "sa28", "37.4.1",
  "sa29", "37.4.2",
  "sa30", "37.4.3"
)

sub_reg_to_map_code &lt;- bind_rows(
  gsa_to_fao |&gt;
    transmute(
      sub_reg_clean = str_remove_all(sub_reg, "\\s"),
      map_code = fao_division
    ),
  black_sea_to_fao |&gt;
    transmute(sub_reg_clean, map_code = fao_division)
)

sea_reg_to_name &lt;- tribble(
  ~Sea_reg,             ~Sea_name,
  "NEAO",               "NEAO",
  "Mediterranean Sea",  "MED",
  "Black Sea",          "Black",
  "Baltic Sea",         "Baltic"
)

european_seas &lt;- sea_reg_to_name$Sea_reg
panel_years &lt;- sort(unique(fish_provisioning$year))

wide_metrics &lt;- function(data, id_cols) {
  widen &lt;- function(value_col, prefix) {
    data |&gt;
      select(all_of(id_cols), year, all_of(value_col)) |&gt;
      pivot_wider(
        names_from = year,
        values_from = all_of(value_col),
        names_prefix = prefix
      )
  }
  widen("landed_tonnes", "tonnes_") |&gt;
    left_join(widen("revenue_thousand_eur", "rent_k_eur_"), by = id_cols) |&gt;
    left_join(widen("revenue_thousand_eur_2008", "rent08_k_eur_"), by = id_cols) |&gt;
    left_join(widen("price_eur_per_kg", "price_eur_kg_"), by = id_cols) |&gt;
    left_join(widen("price_eur_per_kg_2008", "price08_eur_kg_"), by = id_cols) |&gt;
    select(
      all_of(id_cols),
      all_of(paste0("tonnes_", panel_years)),
      all_of(paste0("rent_k_eur_", panel_years)),
      all_of(paste0("rent08_k_eur_", panel_years)),
      all_of(paste0("price_eur_kg_", panel_years)),
      all_of(paste0("price08_eur_kg_", panel_years))
    )
}

by_macro &lt;- fish_provisioning |&gt;
  filter(Sea_reg %in% european_seas) |&gt;
  summarise(
    landed_kg = sum(landed_kg),
    revenue_eur = sum(revenue_eur),
    revenue_eur_2008 = sum(revenue_eur_2008),
    .by = c(year, Sea_reg)
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(sea_reg_to_name, by = "Sea_reg") |&gt;
  mutate(
    landed_tonnes = landed_kg / 1000,
    revenue_thousand_eur = revenue_eur / 1000,
    revenue_thousand_eur_2008 = revenue_eur_2008 / 1000,
    price_eur_per_kg = if_else(landed_kg &gt; 0, revenue_eur / landed_kg, NA_real_),
    price_eur_per_kg_2008 = if_else(
      landed_kg &gt; 0, revenue_eur_2008 / landed_kg, NA_real_
    )
  )

macro_geom &lt;- st_read(gis_file, layer = "sea_regions_macro", quiet = TRUE) |&gt;
  select(Sea_name)

macro_out &lt;- macro_geom |&gt;
  left_join(wide_metrics(by_macro, c("Sea_name", "Sea_reg")), by = "Sea_name")

division_geom &lt;- st_read(gis_file, layer = "sea_regions_division", quiet = TRUE) |&gt;
  select(F_CODE, F_DIVISION, NAME_EN)

division_join_keys &lt;- bind_rows(
  st_drop_geometry(division_geom) |&gt;
    transmute(map_code = F_CODE, F_CODE),
  st_drop_geometry(division_geom) |&gt;
    summarise(n_features = n(), F_CODE = F_CODE[1], .by = F_DIVISION) |&gt;
    filter(n_features == 1) |&gt;
    transmute(map_code = F_DIVISION, F_CODE)
) |&gt;
  distinct(map_code, .keep_all = TRUE)

by_division &lt;- fish_provisioning |&gt;
  filter(Sea_reg %in% european_seas) |&gt;
  left_join(sub_reg_to_map_code, by = "sub_reg_clean") |&gt;
  mutate(map_code = coalesce(map_code, sub_reg_clean)) |&gt;
  inner_join(division_join_keys, by = "map_code") |&gt;
  summarise(
    landed_kg = sum(landed_kg),
    revenue_eur = sum(revenue_eur),
    revenue_eur_2008 = sum(revenue_eur_2008),
    .by = c(year, F_CODE)
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    landed_tonnes = landed_kg / 1000,
    revenue_thousand_eur = revenue_eur / 1000,
    revenue_thousand_eur_2008 = revenue_eur_2008 / 1000,
    price_eur_per_kg = if_else(landed_kg &gt; 0, revenue_eur / landed_kg, NA_real_),
    price_eur_per_kg_2008 = if_else(
      landed_kg &gt; 0, revenue_eur_2008 / landed_kg, NA_real_
    )
  )

division_out &lt;- division_geom |&gt;
  left_join(wide_metrics(by_division, "F_CODE"), by = "F_CODE")

gpkg_out &lt;- file.path(gis_dir, "fish_provisioning.gpkg")
if (file.exists(gpkg_out)) {
  file.remove(gpkg_out)
}

[1] TRUE

st_write(macro_out, gpkg_out, layer = "sea_regions_macro", quiet = TRUE)
st_write(division_out, gpkg_out, layer = "sea_regions_division", quiet = TRUE)

A short coverage check for 2023: how much of the European-sea tonnage found a polygon, and how much of the total was ROW and therefore off the map.

mapped_2023 &lt;- by_division |&gt;
  filter(year == reference_year) |&gt;
  summarise(mapped_tonnes = sum(landed_tonnes))

european_2023 &lt;- fish_provisioning |&gt;
  filter(year == reference_year, Sea_reg %in% european_seas) |&gt;
  summarise(european_tonnes = sum(landed_tonnes))

row_2023 &lt;- fish_provisioning |&gt;
  filter(year == reference_year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    total_tonnes = sum(landed_tonnes),
    row_tonnes = sum(landed_tonnes[Sea_reg == "ROW"])
  )

tibble(
  european_tonnes = european_2023$european_tonnes,
  mapped_tonnes = mapped_2023$mapped_tonnes,
  unmapped_european_tonnes = european_2023$european_tonnes - mapped_2023$mapped_tonnes,
  row_tonnes = row_2023$row_tonnes,
  row_share_pct = 100 * row_2023$row_tonnes / row_2023$total_tonnes
) |&gt;
  gt() |&gt;
  fmt_number(
    c(european_tonnes, mapped_tonnes, unmapped_european_tonnes, row_tonnes),
    decimals = 1
  ) |&gt;
  fmt_number(row_share_pct, decimals = 1)







